Grade 1 cervical strain from sudden braking in low-speed kinetics collision to neck
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Delayed-onset paraspinal stiffness, Diffuse non-midline neck tenderness, Normal thorough neurological examination

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-003
Reason for Visit: Worsening neck pain and stiffness following a motor vehicle collision, requiring urgent neurological clearance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted post-traumatic cervical pain following a rapid acceleration-deceleration mechanism. Urgent clinical evaluation was mandated to exclude cervical spine fracture or cord compromise and prevent potential neurological deterioration before morning clinical services opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My neck got extremely stiff and sore today after someone rear-ended my car yesterday.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports delayed-onset posterior neck stiffness appearing 12 to 24 hours post-collision. The mechanism was a rapid acceleration-deceleration force in a motor vehicle. Patient describes diffuse bilateral tenderness upon palpation of the cervical paraspinal musculature. Active cervical range of motion is mildly restricted, limited by muscular tightness rather than sharp, localized bony pain. Patient notes relief of discomfort with rest and application of local heat.
Functional Status: Independent but avoiding head turning.
Social/Safety Concerns: Driving capacity is currently unsafe due to restricted cervical rotation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, holding head rigidly in a neutral position but in no acute systemic distress.
Physical Exam: Cervical examination shows delayed-onset paraspinal stiffness and diffuse non-midline neck tenderness. Active range of motion is reduced symmetrically by 20 degrees. Complete absence of focal midline spinous process tenderness. A normal thorough neurological examination is confirmed: normal upper extremity motor strength bilaterally, intact and symmetrical deep tendon reflexes, and preservation of normal sensory dermatomes without radicular pain or paresthesias.
Environmental Observations: Patient seated in supportive chair. Heat packs appropriately utilized.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter ibuprofen. No prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 cervical strain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Cervical spine fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of midline spinous tenderness, lack of severe high-risk mechanism (low speed), and intact active range of motion beyond 45 degrees.

Cervical radiculopathy - Ruled out due to: Normal sensory, motor, and reflex examination in bilateral upper limbs.

Traumatic disc herniation - Ruled out due to: Lack of shooting neurogenic pain, weakness, or Lhermitte's sign.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presence of post-collision cervical pain required mandatory evaluation to rule out structural spinal injury. Bedside assessment confirmed the pathology is confined to musculotendinous strain, satisfying the Canadian C-Spine Rules for safe clinical clearance without immediate imaging. Urgent after-hours visitation was necessary to definitively exclude cervical fracture and neurological deficit, providing necessary reassurance and acute muscular spasm management that could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral muscle relaxants and scheduled paracetamol. Routine anteroposterior and lateral cervical spine radiographs recommended only if symptoms fail to improve.
Positive Behavioral Change: Engage in gentle, active neck range of motion exercises within limits of pain to prevent secondary muscular contractures. Reward compliance with faster restoration of driving capacity.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heat packs.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 or present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop weakness in your arms or legs, numbness, tingling, or loss of bowel/bladder control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Assessment is clinical. Latent symptoms or hidden injuries can occasionally manifest later.
Clinical Course: Muscular stiffness typically peaks at 48 hours post-injury before gradually resolving over 1 to 3 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you experience any numbness or pins and needles in your arms, or if the pain becomes sudden and severe in the center of your spine.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Low-grade heat application increases local blood flow, reducing paraspinal muscle spasm and facilitating movement.
Actionable Steps: Avoid rigid neck collars. Turn your head gently side-to-side every hour. Apply heat for 20 minutes at a time.

